On Monday, February 08, 2016 14:31:19 MorphemeAddict wrote:
> Exactly. That's why I specified "to a person who knows neither".
> 
> Aside from the apostrophe/h and more frequent sh/'c' sound, what's the real
> difference? They have the same word shapes, basically the same sounds. Is
> there any distinctive intonation pattern in either language?

I haven't heard much Loglan, so I'm not aware of the intonation pattern. I do 
know, though, that Lojban and Loglan differ in phonotactics. Loglan has words 
like "vizka", "fuacli", and "tsitoa" which contain sequences that don't occur 
in Lojban. ("fuacli" and "tsitoa" are both lujvo.)

